The LaTeX tag for chemical formula in images is typically used to display chemical structures in a clear and readable format. It is often used in scientific documents and presentations to represent chemical compounds. The tag you would use is `\ce{}`, which stands for "chemical equation." This tag is part of the `mhchem` package in LaTeX, which is commonly used for typesetting chemical formulas and reactions.

For example, to display the chemical formula for caffeine, you would write the following LaTeX code:

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{mhchem}
\begin{document}
\ce{C8H10N4O2}
\end{document}
```

When compiled, this code will produce the chemical structure of caffeine, which includes the carbon, hydrogen, nitrogen, and oxygen atoms, as well as the bonds between them.

The LaTeX tag for chemical formula in images is typically used to display chemical structures in a clear and readable format. It is often used in scientific documents and presentations to represent chemical compounds. The tag usually involves using a chemical drawing software or a LaTeX package like `chemfig` or `chemmacros` to create the chemical structure, which is then converted into an image format that can be embedded in a LaTeX document.

